Software License Simplification for OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Price decreases for several operating system user licenses

o  OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 Unlimited Interactive User Licenses and DEC OSF/1
   Server Extension Licenses reflect the new system class structure

o  Users can move OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 Unlimited and Distributed
   Interactive User Licenses between processors with the same hardware
   architecture and operating system

DESCRIPTION

There are now three system classes for OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 Unlimited User
Licenses and DEC OSF/1 Server Extension Licenses. The previous structure of
a different license for each system has been simplified. Systems are now
grouped into classes: Workgroup, Departmental, and Enterprise. (Please see
the "Software License Simplification" article in this issue.)

When users upgrade to another system within the same system class, the
Unlimited User or Server Extension Licenses can be moved to the new system.
There is no need to purchase a new Unlimited User or Server Extension
License. For license upgrade to a higher class, a new license is required,
but users will receive a 75 percent trade-in credit towards the purchase of
the new license.

As previously announced, users can also move their operating system
Distributed Interactive User Licenses between processors with the same
hardware architecture and operating system. This includes OpenVMS VAX,
OpenVMS AXP, and DEC OSF/1 operating systems. In addition, OpenVMS
Interactive User Licenses are sharable across an entire OpenVMS VAXcluster
or an entire OpenVMS AXP cluster.

BENEFITS

o  Fewer model numbers.
o  Simpler ordering.
o  Price reductions for licenses for many models.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

Pricing for software licensing based on the new system class structure for
VAX and Alpha AXP software will be effective worldwide on October 10, 1994.
The single user license price for OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 software will now be
priced exactly the same. This means there will be a decrease in the price
for OpenVMS Distributed Interactive User Licenses. The impact on the price
of the Unlimited Interactive User License will vary, reflecting the broad
price range of the existing licenses.  Price changes range from and increase
of $6,560 to a decrease of $18,250.

