DECserver Network Access Software V1.4

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Supports dial-up connections to Novell LANs from remote PCs via the
   Point-to-Point Protocol

o  Supports both remote node and remote control applications

o  Supports accounting event logging and audit trails

o  Supported on the DECserver 90M, DECserver 900TM, and DECserver 700 models
   with 2 Mbytes of memory and DECserver 90TL upgraded to 4 Mbytes of memory

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

DECserver Network Access Software V1.4 (SPD 46.13), a new release of the
DECserver kernal software, now adds IPX connectivity to Ethernet networks
for remote users, to the currently supported remote access solutions for IP
and Appletalk protocols.

DECserver Network Access Software V1.4 supports the DECserver 900TM, the
DECserver 90M, any DECserver 700 equipped with 2 Mbytes or more of memory,
and the DECserver 90TL upgraded to 4 Mbytes of memory.

The new software will support host multiplexing for attached PC nodes using
the Point-to-Point protocol (PPP), either directly attached to the DECserver
port, or over a dial-up telephone line. Through host multiplexing the
DECserver acts as a proxy agent for as many users as the DECserver has
ports. These users are able to connect to the network as full network peers
(nodes) with access to services as if they were directly attached.

Once the PC establishes a connection to the DECserver system it is able to
forward IPX datagrams to and from Novell NetWare nodes, reachable via an
Ethernet connection, or through another port on a DECserver system.

Client software for the PC is required. Since the DECserver software is
compliant with the PPP standard it will interoperate with any client that
also is compliant. Digital has extensively tested and recommends the
"Wanderlink Universal Client" developed by Funk Software Inc.

The Wanderlink client allows the remote PC to negotiate a PPP link with the
DECserver software. Once the PPP link is established, the Wanderlink client
allows either remote node or remote control style communications between the
remote PC and services on the network.

DECserver Network Access Software V1.4 supports an accounting capability
that provides event logging of significant user actions (e.g., log-ins,
session connects, or password failures). Records of these events can be
useful in supporting capacity planning, audit trails, billing, and
connection trouble shooting. Events can be sent as they occur to a physical
port where they can be displayed on a connected terminal or printer or
redirected to a remote location. To print or display events as they occur
does not require that any DECserver memory be reserved.

DECserver memory can be reserved for storing events so that the DECserver
itself contains a log of accounting events. These events can be browsed via
a SNMP station or via the user interface. An accounting threshold variable
can be set that will notify a harvester application to begin reading entries
before the log is full. These notifications are in the form of SNMP TRAPS.

New Features

o  IPX over PPP -- DECserver Network Access Software V1.4 will now support
   host multiplexing for attached IPX based hosts. Remotely attached users
   look and feel as if locally attached and have access to all services on
   the network. Once a PPP link is established users can also choose to
   operate a remote control application.

o  Accounting -- DECserver Network Access Software V1.4 will now also
   support an accounting application that allows user actions to be viewed
   as they occur or printed for a permanent record. Events stored in
   DECserver memory can be browsed via a SNMP management station or via the
   DECserver user interface.

BENEFITS

The DECserver Network Access Software V1.4 enhances the DECserver
communication server family by providing additional software features that
make the DECserver systems more versatile. The additional software features
protect the user's investment in the DECserver hardware by allowing the
hardware to be used in more diverse network configurations and applications.
The software supports multi-vendor, multi-operating system network access
for LAT, TCP/IP, IPX, and AppleTalk applications.

