Forest 4788 Delivers IBM Mainframe and AS/400 Connectivity

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Integrates DEC OSF/1, ULTRIX, UNIX, and TCP/IP environments with IBM
   mainframe and IBM AS/400 networks

o  Provides direct access from DEC OSF/1 clients and servers, ULTRIX clients
   and servers, UNIX clients and severs, PATHWORKS TCP/IP clients, and
   TELNET terminal servers

o  Requires no layered software on Digital or IBM hosts

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Developed by Forest Computer, a leading manufacturer of IBM interconnect
products, the Forest 4788 Network Resource uses Digital Alpha AXP technology
to deliver IBM interconnect services to DEC OSF/1, ULTRIX, UNIX, PATHWORKS
TCP/IP, and other TCP/IP based environments. The Forest 4788 is a dedicated
turnkey gateway that is completely self-contained; no additional layered
software is required on any of the interconnected hosts. IBM interconnect
services offered by the Forest 4788 include:

o  Digital VT terminal access to IBM mainframe and IBM AS/400 applications
   via the TCP/IP TELNET facility.

o  IBM 3270 and IBM 5250 workstation access to DEC OSF/1, ULTRIX, and UNIX
   applications via standard IBM protocols.

o  Attended or unattended file transfer.

o  Automatic queue-to-queue printer sharing.

o  Remote job submission to Digital and IBM systems.

o  Program-to-program communications in realtime.

o  Support for Digital PATHWORKS TCP/IP workstations.

o  Support for IBM PC and CA/400 workstations.

On the Digital side of the network, the Forest 4788 supports the TCP/IP
suite of protocols and utilities. No additional layered software is required
in any of the DEC OSF/1, ULTRIX, UNIX, or PATHWORKS systems. Instead, the
Forest 4788 is accessed using standard network services such as the File
Transfer Protocol (FTP), TELNET, and other core TCP/IP utilities.

On the IBM side of the network, IBM mainframe interaction is performed using
standard System Network Architecture (SNA) and Network Job Entry (NJE)
services. Similarly, IBM AS/400 traffic is accommodated using native
Advanced Peer-to-Peer Networking (APPN) and Advanced Program-to-Program
Communication (APPC) connections. As in the case of the Digital systems, no
additional layered products are required on the IBM mainframes or IBM AS/400
systems. All standard IBM operating systems are supported, and a single
Forest 4788 can support both IBM mainframe and IBM AS/400 traffic.

BENEFITS

o  Compatibility with TCP/IP networks.
o  Direct access from application systems, servers, workstations, PCs, and
   terminal servers.
o  Operating system independence.
o  No layered software required on any of the user's clients, servers, or
   application systems.
o  All communications overhead off-loaded from clients, servers, and
   application systems.
o  High performance, ease of use, reliability, and cost effectiveness.
o  The high quality of Forest's support services, which consistently receive
   high ratings in customer satisfaction surveys.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The Alpha AXP based version of the Forest 4788 will be available in October
1994.

